  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 119 PM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nUpper high slowly drifts east across NM Mon/Tue, with a\ncontinued slow uptick in moisture across srn CO both days. As a\nresult, thunderstorm coverage, especially over the mountains, \nshould increase, with deep enough moisture for at least pockets \nof measurable rainfall across the higher terrain. Weaker \nconvection possible on the plains both days, with still some dry\nlow level air and large t/td spreads to overcome away from the\nmountains, so more of a wind than rain threat here. With \nincreased moisture/clouds, max temps will drift downward a few \ndegf both days, though readings will still be near/slightly over\n100f on the plains each afternoon. Held off on a heat advisory \nfor Mon as current forecast temps are slightly below heat \nthresholds, though Springs/Pueblo/Canon City will be close. \n\nCenter of the upper high then makes a more substantial eastward\nshift into the srn plains from Wed into the weekend, opening the\ndoor for moisture to rotate northward from nrn Mexico through\nthe Desert SW into CO. Late week cold front on the plains will\nalso provide a surge of low level moisture to areas along and\neast of the mountains as well, especially in the Thu/Fri \ntimeframe. Expect widespread showers/thunderstorms from Wed into\nthe weekend as a result, with at least some activity lingering \nwell into the night under the moist plume. Locally heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding definitely a concern, especially \neastern mountains/I-25 corridor Thu/Fri behind the cold front. \nTemps will continue to cool back toward seasonal averages or \nslightly below by the end of the week as clouds and precip knock\nback the heat through next weekend.",
